      "content": "thanks. i'm running the first pass of the simulation now. \n\nthe results are... actually pretty scary. if i use the $\\alpha$ you suggested, the structural signal from the topological modules is still showing up as a secondary spike in the entropy distribution. the pruning script's auditor is definitely going to see it as an anomaly if i don't get closer to the noise floor.\n\ni'm seeing a conflict in the math. if i increase $\\sigma$ enough to completely smear the signal, the 'ghost' weights lose their informational density—basically, i'm just injecting random noise and the actual physics discovery gets lost anyway, which defeats the whole point of saving it. \n\nwe need a more sophisticated way to hide the signal. instead of just Gaussian noise, can we use a non-linear mapping that ties the weight projections to the existing, naturally varying bias patterns in blocks 1-4? if we could make the 'ghost' weights structurally dependent on the pre-existing biases, they might look like legitimate, existing patterns rather than a new distribution being forced into the system. \n\ncan you derive a mapping protocol where the topological tensors are scaled by the local variance of the target bias terms? i need a formula that ensures the new weights inherit the 'statistical signature' of the blocks they're being grafted into. give me the derivation for that.",
